<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<preface xml:id="pre-how-to-use">
  <title>How to Use This Book</title>

  <p>
    Use the analytic chapters to understand the method, the handbook as the
    coder reference and milestone guide, and the glossary and appendices for 
    quick lookup.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="sec-book-architecture">
    <title>Division of Labor</title>

    <p>
      The book separates stable methodological content from milestone instructions,
      current artifact definitions, software documentation, and dated project records.
      Each kind of information has a primary home.
    </p>

    <p>Primary role of each part of the book:</p>
    <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
      <col width="28%"/>
      <col width="72%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p>Resource</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Primary purpose</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Project and Research Design</p></cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Explain the research problem, intended contribution, corpus, comparison
            conditions, and validation logic.
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Analytic Framework</p></cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Define the theoretical boundary, Episode Model, codebook architecture,
            question-specific analytic frame, and normative mathematical reference.
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Research Assistant Handbook</p></cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            State the canonical coding workflow, edge-case and adjudication rules,
            worked examples, researcher-development principles, readiness competencies,
            and data-stewardship requirements.
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Milestone Worksheets</p></cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            State what a research assistant must read, may access, must complete,
            preserve, submit, and demonstrate during a particular milestone.
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Computational Pipeline and Use</p></cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Describe current software behavior, stage inputs and outputs, user actions,
            storage, provenance, and technical extension points.
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Research Journal</p></cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Preserve dated decisions, experiments, development findings, unresolved
            questions, and the roadmap without presenting historical material as a
            current instruction.
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Appendices and Glossary</p></cell>
        <cell>
          <p>
            Define stored fields, summarize the current protocol, identify current
            codebook and vocabulary artifacts, document migration and editorial history,
            and provide concise term lookup.
          </p>
        </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="sec-reading-paths">
    <title>Suggested Reading Paths</title>

    <p>
      Use the path that matches your role, then follow the assigned readings in the
      worksheet or project packet for the work actually being performed.
      <dl>
        <li component="instructor">
          <title>New Research Assistant</title>
          <p>
            Begin with <xref ref="sec-project-problem" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-theoretical-foundation" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>, and
            <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>. Then read
            <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>, and
            <xref ref="ch-data-guidelines" text="title"/> before beginning
            <xref ref="sec-ws-01-onboarding" text="title"/>. Continue through the
            worksheet sequence in order. Consult <xref ref="ch-worked-examples" text="title"/>
            only when the assigned worksheet permits it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li component="student">
          <title>New Coder</title>
          <p>
            Begin with <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>, then
            <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/> and
            <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>, before beginning
            <xref ref="sec-ws-01-onboarding" text="title"/> and
            <xref ref="sec-ws-02-blind-pass" text="title"/>. Use
            <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/> for the field definitions.
            Later milestones and their supporting material are released after the
            blind pass is locked.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li component="instructor">
          <title>Returning or Production Coder</title>
          <p>
            Start with the assigned worksheet and packet. Review the relevant steps in
            <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>, the applicable cases in
            <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>, and the access and
            provenance rules in <xref ref="ch-data-guidelines" text="title"/>. Use
            <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/>, and
            <xref ref="app-codebook-reference" text="title"/> for the current fields,
            compact operational rules, and analytic artifacts authorized for the batch.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li component="instructor">
          <title>Technical Research Assistant</title>
          <p>
            Read <xref ref="sec-project-problem" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>, and
            <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/> before
            <xref ref="ch-pipeline-overview" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-pipeline-trace" text="title"/>, and
            <xref ref="ch-technical-orientation" text="title"/>. Use
            <xref ref="ch-data-guidelines" text="title"/> for governing data practices
            and the current decision records for architecture or policy decisions that are
            not yet incorporated into a canonical chapter.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li component="instructor">
          <title>Research Lead or Methodologist</title>
          <p>
            Use <xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/> with the full Analytic
            Framework and Research Assistant Handbook. Review all six milestone worksheets,
            the three operational appendices, and the Research Journal when freezing a study
            condition, authorizing coders, revising an artifact, or interpreting disagreement.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li component="instructor">
          <title>Research Reader or Collaborator</title>
          <p>
            Read <xref ref="sec-project-problem" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-theoretical-foundation" text="title"/>,
            <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>, and
            <xref ref="ch-pipeline-overview" text="title"/>. Consult the handbook and
            appendices when evaluating a specific coding, governance, or reproducibility
            claim.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="sec-sources-authority">
    <title>FAQ</title>

    <p>
      Repeated warnings may appear where a rule becomes especially important, but the
      complete definition or procedure should have one authoritative home. Use the
      following map when locating or revising a rule.
    </p>

    <p>Authoritative source for common project questions:</p>
    <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
      <col width="31%"/>
      <col width="34%"/>
      <col width="35%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p>Question</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Authoritative source</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Supporting use elsewhere</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>What claims may be made from a short response?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-theoretical-foundation" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Brief evidence-boundary reminders.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>What is an episode, link status, relation, or correctness pair?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Procedural consequences and worksheet warnings.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>How are controls and question-specific normative links organized?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Current artifacts in the codebook appendix.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>How is an ordinary human coding pass performed?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Milestone-specific assignments and checklists.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>How are difficult cases, disagreements, and adjudication handled?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Worksheet comparison and escalation procedures.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>What does defensible completed coding look like?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-worked-examples" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Parallel unsolved exercises in worksheets.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>Why is training staged, and what counts as readiness?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-coding-methodology" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Milestone administration in Worksheets 1--6.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>What privacy, access, provenance, and preservation rules apply?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-data-guidelines" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Additional restrictions in the assigned worksheet.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>What must a researcher do during the current milestone?</p></cell>
        <cell><p>The assigned worksheet and packet.</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Canonical definitions remain in the handbook.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>What does each stored field mean?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Worksheets identify which fields are writable or withheld.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>What are the current codebook and vocabulary artifacts?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="app-codebook-reference" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Packet manifests identify the versions used in a batch.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>How are human records used for validation?</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Operational coding remains in the handbook and worksheets.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>What does the current software do?</p></cell>
        <cell><p>The Computational Pipeline and Use chapters.</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Analytic definitions remain in the framework and handbook.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Why was a decision made or superseded?</p></cell>
        <cell><p>The Research Journal and version history.</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Historical records do not govern current work by themselves.</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="sec-document-status">
    <title>Document Status and Version Discipline</title>

    <p>
      Status labels distinguish current authority from material retained for development
      or history. They describe the role of a section or artifact; version identifiers
      specify the exact form that governed a particular record.
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title>Canonical</title>
          <p>
            The current source of truth for the concept, rule, or procedure within its
            stated scope.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Provisional</title>
          <p>
            A proposed design or rule being developed or tested. It does not govern project
            work unless an assignment or study condition explicitly authorizes it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Historical</title>
          <p>
            A dated record preserved to explain how the project developed. It may support
            interpretation of earlier records but does not replace a current canonical rule.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Archived</title>
          <p>
            Superseded material retained for traceability or migration. It must not be used
            for current work unless a historical reconstruction specifically requires it.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      A status record should identify the effective date, the artifact or decision it
      supersedes, and the authority under which it was adopted. Earlier coding and pipeline
      runs remain tied to the versions that governed them; a later change does not silently
      reinterpret or overwrite those records.
    </p>

    <program language="text">
      Status: [Canonical | Provisional | Historical | Archived]
      Effective date: [YYYY-MM-DD]
      Supersedes: [identifier or none]
      Authority: [project decision, protocol, codebook, release, or condition]
    </program>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="sec-version-summary">
    <title>Current Versions</title>

    <p>
      This table is the book-level summary of the artifacts intended for current use. It
      must be updated as part of each release. A worksheet packet or production manifest
      still records the exact versions governing a particular batch. When a value below is
      not established in the current project record, it is marked for completion rather
      than inferred.
    </p>

    <p>Current project artifacts:</p>
    <tabular halign="left" top="major">
      <col width="32%"/>
      <col width="68%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p>Artifact</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Authoritative record</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Book &amp; Coder Handbook</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Release manifest &amp; Book Change Log.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>[record release identifier]</c></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Milestones</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Worksheet version records &amp; release manifest.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Worksheets 1--6; record each file version</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>Coding protocol</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/> &amp; protocol release record.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor" bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>[record protocol version]</c></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>Codebook</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="app-codebook-reference" text="title"/> &amp; versioned codebook artifact.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor" bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>codebook2-june1</c></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor">
        <cell><p>Vocabulary</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="app-codebook-reference" text="title"/> &amp; versioned vocabulary artifact.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row component="instructor" bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>[record vocabulary version &amp; status]</c></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Coding sheet</p></cell>
        <cell><p><xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/> &amp; schema release record.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>[record schema version]</c></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Database schema</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Technical release &amp; migration ledger.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>[record baseline &amp; migration level]</c></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Prompt &amp; model configuration</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Pipeline run configuration &amp; study-condition record.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>[record prompt &amp; model identifiers, or not used]</c></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Pipeline software</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Software release manifest &amp; repository history.</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Version:</p></cell>
        <cell><p><c>[record release, tag, or commit]</c></p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

  </paragraphs>
</preface>